<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Souvanna Phouma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1901–1984</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Souvanna Phouma was a Lao statesman of the royal family. A member of the Lao Issara nationalist government in 1945, he later served several times as prime minister of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> between 1951 and 1975, seeking neutrality and coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>, led by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>. He stayed on as adviser after the communist takeover of 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/souvanna-phouma.jpg" alt="Souvanna Phouma" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marion S. Trikosko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vice_President_Spiro_Agnew_and_Prince_Souvanna_Phouma_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
